{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789892223,"nonce":"S9gOMbG3JVLxfb/hZQ63YlahFSc=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABSNHTWg","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/141","origin":"github"},"title":"Extending fem.solve(checkpoint=) beyond the adaptive march costs end-to-end reverse-mode AD","message":"## Context\n\n`fem.solve(checkpoint=jno.solve.checkpoint(path, ...))` (branch `feat/weld-bridge`) writes a march's\nframes to disk as they are produced, drops them from memory, and can restart a killed run. Today it is\nwired **only** for the adaptive moving-mesh march (`adapt=jno.solve.remesh(alpha=...)`), and\n`checkpoint=` on any other solve raises `NotImplementedError`.\n\nThat limit is not arbitrary: the adaptive march is the only driver with a **host-side** loop. It steps in\nchunks of `every` and decides reconnection on the host between chunks, so there is a natural boundary at\nwhich to write. Every other iterative driver is a single **compiled** loop, deliberately:\n\n| driver | form | why compiled |\n|---|---|---|\n| plain transient (`u.t`) | `lax.scan` — `backend_blocks.py:646` | reverse-mode differentiable |\n| BDF2 / adaptive-`dt` | `lax.scan` — `timeschemes.py:234`, `:430` | fixed trip count keeps AD working |\n| load path (`tau=`) | one `lax.scan` — `history_march.py:164` | \"differentiable end to end\" |\n| Newton | `lax.while_loop` — `newton_krylov.py:122,194,229` | data-dependent trip count |\n\n## The tradeoff\n\nTo checkpoint those, the compiled loop has to be **host-chunked**: run `every` steps per call instead of\none call for the whole grid. Arithmetically that is free — scanning `k` steps then the next `k` is the\nsame as scanning `2k`, verified bit-identical over 1401 frames on the adaptive march (max |Δstate| = 0,\nmax |Δpoints| = 0).\n\n**What it costs: end-to-end reverse-mode differentiability through the march.** A chained sequence of\nscans is not one scan; the gradient would have to be assembled across chunk boundaries, and the\n`history_march` docstring calls out single-scan AD as a property worth keeping.\n\n## Proposal\n\n- Host-chunking must be **opt-in**, active only when `checkpoint=` is passed, so the default paths and\n  their AD behaviour are untouched.\n- `fem.solve(checkpoint=...)` combined with differentiating through the solve should **raise**, not\n  silently return a wrong or partial gradient.\n- Document the limitation on `jno.solve.checkpoint`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **Newton needs nothing new.** Resuming a nonlinear solve from iterate *k* is a warm start, which is\n  already `fem.solve(x0=...)`. Persisting an iterate and passing it back as `x0` *is* checkpointing there.\n- **Naming hazard:** `jax.checkpoint` is already used in this codebase (`backend_blocks.py:646`) and means\n  gradient *rematerialisation* — unrelated to `jno.solve.checkpoint` (disk checkpointing). The docstring\n  should say so explicitly.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThree separate long runs were lost to OOM/kills during a laser-melt study, one at 91 % (19968 of 22000\nsteps) after half an hour of correct physics, because a march returns its trajectory only when `solve()`\nreturns.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}